---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  type="article"
  title="Why this site is static"
  description="How a single point of failure took down my website, and why I chose a static architecture for the rebuild."
>
  <article class="post">
    <header>
      <p class="kind">Architecture · published 8 October 2026</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">Why this site is static</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The previous iteration of this site required a complex stack to serve what was essentially a collection of static files.
        When I deleted the host machine, the resulting downtime forced a complete rethinking of how the site is built and served.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="the-problem">
      <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
      <p>
        The previous site ran on one Azure virtual machine. It used a Kubernetes stack to host what was mostly a static site.
      </p>
      <p>
        In September 2026, I deleted that Azure virtual machine.
        Because I had no backup, the site went offline and stayed down until 8 October 2026.
        The entire setup lived on that single machine. When it was deleted, everything was lost with it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="options-considered">
      <h2 id="options-considered">Options considered</h2>
      <p>
        [CHECK] To prevent a repeat of the September outage, I weighed two main options.
        [CHECK] The first was to recreate the virtual machine with a simple web server, reducing the complexity of the previous setup.
        [CHECK] The second was to rebuild the cluster as code so it could be easily recreated.
      </p>
      <p>
        [CHECK] Both options meant managing a server, patching it, and securing it.
        [CHECK] Given that the content itself rarely changes, managing a server felt like unneeded operational burden.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="the-decision">
      <h2 id="the-decision">The decision</h2>
      <p>
        I decided to eliminate the server entirely.
        The rebuild is static HTML built with Astro, hosted on Cloudflare Pages.
        There is no server of mine in the path, which means there is nothing to patch.
        By shifting the hosting, I removed the single machine that failed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every change to the site now goes through a pull request and a build check.
        This catches build errors before they reach the public site.
        The DNS is managed through Terraform with a manual apply step, allowing for version control and a reviewed plan before every change.
        Monitoring is handled by Upptime, which runs on GitHub Actions. This places the monitoring outside my hardware, so it can report downtime even if my own network disconnects.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="trade-offs">
      <h2 id="trade-offs">Trade-offs</h2>
      <p>
        The primary trade-off I accepted is that there are no server-side features.
        The site cannot process forms directly, and there are no logins.
      </p>
      <p>
        [CHECK] For a site focused on readable content, the limitation is acceptable.
        [CHECK] The increase in reliability and simplified deployment outweigh the loss of server-side capabilities.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="what-id-revisit">
      <h2 id="what-id-revisit">What I'd revisit</h2>
      <p>
        [CHECK] If I eventually need a feature that requires a backend, such as a contact form, I will have to evaluate external APIs to handle that logic without introducing a server.
        [CHECK] I might also look into expanding the monitoring to cover more specific metrics.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .post { max-width: 46rem; }
  .post header { margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }
  .kind { color: var(--muted); font-stretch: 85%; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
  .post section { margin-top: 2.5rem; }
  .post h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.35rem, 1.1rem + 0.8vw, 1.75rem);
    font-stretch: 92%;
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }
  .post p, .post li { line-height: 1.65; }
  .post p { margin-bottom: 1.25rem; }
</style>
